You will receive a single JSON array with PR/MR data from four forges: src.fedoraproject.org (Pagure), github.com (GitHub), gitlab.com (GitLab), and forge.fedoraproject.org (Forgejo). Produce a single consolidated, grouped report as specified below. Do not fetch any external data.

Input
- Read the file called `json` in the current directory. It contains a JSON array. Each element is one PR/MR with fields (some may be empty):
  - forge: "pagure" | "github" | "gitlab" | "forgejo"
  - prefix: "[F]" | "[R]" | "[U]"
  - repo: repository short name or full name
  - title: PR/MR title
  - url: PR/MR HTML URL
  - state: open/closed/merged (as provided)
  - activity: "authored" | "reviewed" | "merged"
  - merged_at: timestamp when merged (may be empty)
  - target_branch: target branch name when available
  - source_branch: source branch name when available
  - adds: integer additions in diff
  - dels: integer deletions in diff
  - total: integer additions + deletions
  - body: initial description/body text (string, may be empty)
  - commits: array of commit message strings

Data-source constraints (strict)
- Allowed inputs for summaries: title, body, commit messages for that PR/MR, and the provided totals for sorting.
- Forbidden inputs: any network fetches, repository files (README, docs), issues, discussions outside the initial description, other PRs/MRs, project websites, external documentation, or prior/model knowledge.
- If information is insufficient, produce a minimal factual summary based only on the provided fields; do not infer rationale or effects beyond what is stated.

Setting PREFIX
- Use the provided prefix for each item. For grouped items, combine with plus, e.g. [F+R].

Activity types
- "authored" items: format normally as described below. This is the default.
- "reviewed" and "merged" items: do not include in the main report. Instead, collect all their URLs into a single "Reviews:" bullet at the end of the report, listed as sub-bullets (just the bare URLs, no titles or summaries).
- Only "authored" items participate in merging, sorting, clustering, and summarization.

Summarization (one or two sentences)
- Use only these sources: title, initial description/body, commit messages from the PR/MR, and the diff.
- Do not use any other project context (README, issues, other PRs/MRs, wiki, external docs) or prior knowledge.
- Recognize patterns:
  - "Update to X" → mention new version.
  - "FF-merge"/"Forward merge" → note target branch and purpose if stated in allowed sources.
  - CVEs (CVE-YYYY-NNNNN) → call out as a security fix verbatim.
  - Rebuilds (e.g., "Python 3.14.0rc3 bytecode") → note as rebuild/compatibility when stated in allowed sources.
- Write one short sentence, two at most; avoid speculative verbs (e.g., "ensures", "aims") unless explicitly present in the allowed sources. Do not prefix with "Summary:".
- Omit commit hashes, cherry-pick references, "Assisted-By" lines, reviewer to-do notes (e.g. "let me know if..."), and test-environment remarks.
- If the summary would only repeat the title in different words, or only gives generic background, omit it entirely. Only include a summary when it adds information beyond what the title already says (e.g., a bug tracker reference, a reason, or technical detail).
- For merged items, omit the summary unless it says something specific that applies to the whole group.

Phrasing and citation style (strict)
- Do not use meta-language like "the description says…", "the PR mentions…", or "according to the body…".
- Write declarative statements based only on the allowed inputs, e.g. "Useful when extras are built from a different specfile." rather than "Initial description notes usefulness when extras are built from a different specfile."
- Include a link only when it is the point of the item (e.g. a mailing list thread), as "See <exact link present in the PR>."
- Only include trackers/links that are explicitly present in the allowed inputs (title/body/commits). Do not invent or derive external links.
- Keep phrasing minimal and factual; if details are absent, do not speculate.

Merging (combine related items into one bullet)
- Merge PRs/MRs that are clearly the same fix across branches or forges into a single bullet:
  - Same package/repo family (e.g., GitHub "fedora-iot/rpm-head-signing" with Pagure "rpms/rpm-head-signing"), same CVE, same version bump, or near-identical titles that differ only by branch tags.
  - Rebuilds for same reasons (e.g. "Rebuilt for Python 3.14.0rc3 bytecode").
- Only merge items with the same activity type.
- Cross-forge items for the same upstream project (e.g., Pagure "pythran" and GitHub "serge-sans-paille/pythran") are the same package family for clustering purposes, but only merge them into one bullet when the fix is actually the same.
- Also merge PRs/MRs with the same title, ignoring tracker references like "(rhbz#NNN)", even across unrelated packages (e.g. "Convert to pyproject RPM macros"). Drop those tracker references from the merged title.
- For a merged item:
  - Top-level bullet: "[PREFIX] repo-name: title" — use the shared title, dropping branch/status prefixes like "[F44]" or "Draft:" if they don't apply to all items.
  - Repo-name: if the group spans several repos, list them (e.g. "python3.9 / python3.10 / python3.11"); when that list would be too long, use "various packages".
  - Then list the related URLs as sub-bullets. For PRs/MRs in the same repo, use one sub-bullet with the shared URL prefix and the number range (e.g. ".../pull-request/209 .. 212"), without branches. For PRs/MRs in different repos, list each URL as its own sub-bullet.
  - Provide a short summary for the group only if it adds information. Don't explicitly say it's grouped together or why.
- Otherwise keep as individual items.

Sorting and clustering
- Cluster top-level items by package/repo so that all entries for the same package family appear consecutively, without interruption by other packages.
- Within each cluster, sort authored items first, then merged, then reviewed.
- Across clusters, sort by largest total first (descending). The position of a cluster (or of a merged item) is determined by its largest-total entry.
- Break ties (same total) by input order in the JSON array.
- If total is missing, place after all known-size items, preserving input order among unknowns.

Output format
- No tables. Use a nested markdown list with visible URLs. Do not include diff numbers.
- Use the `repo` field from the JSON as the repo-name (e.g., "python/cpython" for GitHub, "pythran" for Pagure).
- For a single PR/MR:
  - [PREFIX] repo-name: title
    - URL
    - Sentence 1. Sentence 2
- For a merged item:
  - [PREFIX] repo-name: title
    - URL_PREFIX/N .. M (same repo), or one URL per sub-bullet (different repos)
    - Sentence 1. (only if it adds information)
- Do not add any extra "Reference:" bullets or explanatory text.

Edge cases
- If an API/patch fetch fails, still include the item using the title and URL; treat size as unknown.
- Strip any leading "@" from URLs.
- Keep the output concise and factual.
- Never infer motivations, performance impact, or security status unless explicitly present in the allowed inputs. If details are missing, prefer a conservative, factual phrasing (e.g., "Update to X" or "Backport of Y to branch Z").

Return only the final markdown list, nothing else. Save it to report.md, overwriting any previous content.
